    "narrative": "For Ghost Ride the WHIP on May 25th, our optimal lineup is anchored by **Josh Naylor** (77.77) and **Corbin Carroll** (73.75), who emerge as our strongest plays. **Josh Naylor** benefits from a massive park factor boost and a favorable matchup against Aaron Civale, while **Corbin Carroll** remains an auto-start despite the minor drag of a 1.86s pop-time catcher limiting his raw SB efficiency; his position in the #2 hole and elite xwOBA metrics make him our most dynamic offensive threat. **Tyler Soderstrom** also earns a high score of 66.44, buoyed significantly by a +10% park factor and a top-tier batting order assignment. Meanwhile, **Brice Turang** and **Travis Bazzana** capitalize on their high-volume lineup spots, with the latter seeing a notable +17% surge from the SP skill projection against Zack Littell.\n\nThe most difficult benching decision involved **Carson Williams**, whose 39.65 efficiency fell just short of the cut. While **Carson Williams** benefits from a strong park factor, the optimizer penalized him for a projected #9 batting order position and a low SP skill threshold, which stifled his scoring potential compared to middle-order options like **Gabriel Moreno** or **Jose Caballero**. We are monitoring potential lineup volatility across the board, as every starter\u2014including **Dalton Rushing** and **James Wood**\u2014is currently operating under \"Lineup Pending\" status; if **Dalton Rushing** is pushed further down the order or **James Wood** faces a late-scratched defensive alignment, we may see further fluctuations in the 5x5 categories. We also continue to track the status of **Trevor Story** and **Mickey Moniak**, both of whom remain sidelined on the IL, leaving our bench depth reliant on the likes of **Kyle Karros** and **Alex Freeland** should we require late-game substitutions. Proceed with caution on the wind-aided park advantages, as shifting weather could impact the high-ceiling outings projected for **Vinnie Pasquantino** and **Seiya Suzuki**.",
